  "full_text": "I think it was during the Theresa May election, I gave a pretend speech. As Prime Minister, I will restore Britain to world domination as the musical superpower. So what you have to do is you have to make them poor. See, because that's where it comes from. You've got to make them poor, you've got to abuse them. You need something to sing about, don't you? You're too fat and happy. You got nothing to sing about. There was a time when Britain, when the, like the band Cream, you know, you needed to have something to say. And they were basically priests. The rock music people were priests. You know, a lot of them, not all of them, but I mean, there were a lot of them who were, you know, they were the priests, you know, for that culture. And even today, I'm kind of surprised. I'm like, wow. You know, I kind of went off into the computer nerd culture, which is largely atheist. And then I look back at the rock music culture and I see these, like the leftover, you know, white. And I'm kind of like surprised at how religious they are. Like how, if you look at the rock bands through the... through the, I don't know, into the 2000s and stuff. It's kind of interesting that they're consistently religious. I think that's cool. It puts me to shame a little bit. Not really. But you'll find the, I guess the more, is that the more normal people? I don't know the demographics anymore really, but like the... The computer atheist nerds are all kind of I understand that but I don't know I don't know what's the breakdown you know because there are a certain number of What are they more working-class people that are kind of like that? You know where are those people now and stuff anyway, but it's kind of interesting a lot There's the the rock music groups were the priests, you know",
